{
  "id": "u01-l02",
  "unit": "u01-basics",
  "title": "I and you",
  "status": "draft",
  "tip": "Bengali has more than one 'you'. Use **আপনি** (*apni*) with elders, strangers, teachers and anyone you want to show respect to. Use **তুমি** (*tumi*) with friends, younger people and partners. When unsure, use আপনি. (A third, very intimate **তুই** comes in Unit 2.)\n\nThere is no 'am / is / are' in simple present sentences and no 'a / the': **আমি ছাত্র** (*ami chhatro*) = 'I (am a) student'. Student has a male form **ছাত্র** and a female form **ছাত্রী** (*chhatri*); **শিক্ষক** (*shikkhok*) is 'teacher' for anyone.",
  "vocab": [
    {
      "id": "ami",
      "bn": "আমি",
      "tr": "ami",
      "en": "I"
    },
    {
      "id": "tumi",
      "bn": "তুমি",
      "tr": "tumi",
      "en": "you (familiar)",
      "notes": "For friends, younger people, partners."
    },
    {
      "id": "apni",
      "bn": "আপনি",
      "tr": "apni",
      "en": "you (polite)",
      "notes": "Safe default with anyone older or unfamiliar."
    },
    {
      "id": "chhatro",
      "bn": "ছাত্র",
      "tr": "chhatro",
      "en": "student (male)",
      "notes": "Often used for any student regardless of gender."
    },
    {
      "id": "chhatri",
      "bn": "ছাত্রী",
      "tr": "chhatri",
      "en": "student (female)"
    },
    {
      "id": "shikkhok",
      "bn": "শিক্ষক",
      "tr": "shikkhok",
      "en": "teacher",
      "notes": "The conjunct ক্ষ is pronounced 'kkh'. A female teacher is শিক্ষিকা (shikkhika), though শিক্ষক is increasingly used for all."
    }
  ],
